      "title": "Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "4a8ff9f0-aa9a-5dc8-be16-b8d68d0d421c",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3x+(3)y?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(2,2)$,$(-3,1)$,$(1,1)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,2)}=3(2)+(3)(2)=12, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-3,1)}=3(-3)+(3)(1)=-6, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,1)}=3(1)+(3)(1)=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172038/review-4-policy-2-64bbd1f805696f06/cli-assets/ecf6fa52fd47da762c5fb722339486dd942995c79f9e7f317f0c80e55152bf76.png)",
